# Design — fixSessionHealthBannerNoise

## Root cause (confirmed)

`compactPromptMessages` (index.ts:180) truncates oversized tool outputs / diff patches **in place** — it replaces `message.info` patch text and `part.output` with a 4K excerpt. Consequence: on the turn where compaction first happens, `compactedDiffs > 0` and the messages-transform hook sets `state.lastSessionHealthIssue` (index.ts:1334). On subsequent turns the already-truncated text is < 24K, so `compactedDiffs == 0` and **no new issue is created** — but `healthSection` (system-block.ts:191) re-emits the banner every turn because nothing ever clears `lastSessionHealthIssue`. That is the every-turn noise.

## Implementation strategy

### 1. Add `surfaced` to the issue shape

- `plugin/src/utils/system-block.ts:42` — add optional `surfaced?: boolean` to the `SessionHealthIssue` interface (the module-local mirror).
- `plugin/src/index.ts` — the two issue-construction sites (session.error at ~1068, message-history at ~1334) leave `surfaced` unset (defaults to falsy) so a freshly-recorded issue always emits once.

### 2. Gate emission in `healthSection`

```ts
function healthSection(input: AssembleSystemBlockInput): string | null {
  const issue = input.state.lastSessionHealthIssue;
  if (!issue) return null;
  // session.error stays sticky (safety-critical). message-history is
  // one-shot: suppress re-emission once it has been surfaced.
  if (issue.kind === "message-history" && issue.surfaced) return null;
  return formatSessionHealthBanner(issue, input.state.activeChange.id);
}
```

### 3. Report emission from the assembler, mark surfaced in the hook

Follow the existing `consumedWisdomPrompt` pattern (system-block.ts:30-31 "caller owns clearing per-turn volatile state"):

- The assembler `applyAdvSystemBlock` result gains `surfacedMessageHistoryHealth: boolean` — true when it emitted a `message-history` health banner this assembly.
- In the `experimental.chat.system.transform` hook (index.ts:1298, right after the `result` is obtained), add:

```ts
if (
  result.surfacedMessageHistoryHealth &&
  state.lastSessionHealthIssue?.kind === "message-history"
) {
  state.lastSessionHealthIssue.surfaced = true;
}
```

This emits the banner on the first assembly (surfaced still falsy), then marks it so the next assembly suppresses it. A new compaction event replaces `lastSessionHealthIssue` with a fresh (unsurfaced) issue → re-emits once.

### 4. Documentation (SC3)

Add a concise "one OpenCode session per major change" hygiene note to the Resume Freshness Advisory section (ADV_INSTRUCTIONS.md) — the accumulation root cause is chaining multiple archives in a single long session.

## LBP decisions

- **Why one-shot for message-history but sticky for session.error**: a compaction warning is informational and stable — repeating it every turn is noise once seen. A `session.error` sets BLOCKED status and is safety-critical; it must persist until the error clears (DONT1).
- **Why assembler-reports + hook-mutates instead of mutating in the formatter**: `healthSection` is a pure formatter by contract (system-block.ts:30-31). State mutation belongs in the hook, mirroring `consumedWisdomPrompt`.
- **Why `surfaced` and not clearing the issue outright**: clearing would lose the issue for diagnostics (`resolveStatus` reads `lastSessionHealthIssue?.kind === "session.error"` for BLOCKED). A flag suppresses the banner without erasing the record.

## Risk

- Low. Additive optional field; pure formatter gate; one hook mutation. Backward-compatible when `surfaced` absent (AC4).
- Test surface: `system-block.test.ts` health-banner tests need a case for surfaced-suppression (AC1), fresh-issue re-emit (AC2), and session.error sticky (AC3).

## No spec-law change

Behavioral UX refinement to an existing banner; no capability spec scenario is added or modified.
